WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.040
Hey, Wisecrack.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.380
Jared here again -- this time to discuss the
punching bag of the DC Universe.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.220
No, not that one; This one.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:17.260
"It's cherry."

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:25.400
Now since everyone’s already beat the film
up for its shameless franchise plugging...its

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.640
slow plotting...and well...

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:30.840
"Save Martha."

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:35.000
that, we're going
to focus on something else - Batman v. Superman’s

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.740
ideological failings.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.420
So let’s see if it “bleeds.”

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:39.320
"You will."

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.260
Welcome to a Wisecrack Edition on Batman v.
Superman - What Went Wrong?

00:00:43.269 --> 00:00:44.870
And of course, spoilers ahead.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:50.519
One of the biggest challenges facing the architects
of the DCEU is: how to make Superman a relatable

00:00:50.519 --> 00:00:51.519
character?

00:00:51.519 --> 00:00:55.879
After all, it’s hard to identify with an
indestructible, self-less, god-like figure.

00:00:55.879 --> 00:00:57.059
So what was their solution?

00:00:57.059 --> 00:00:59.249
Well, a pretty smart one if you ask me.

00:00:59.249 --> 00:01:03.600
They kind of did what was done in the Last
Temptation of Christ- took a benevolent messiah

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.580
figure and emphasized his vulnerabilities,
flaws, and most importantly- doubts.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.260
"I'm a man like everybody else."

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:10.860
"Quiet."

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:16.520
Doubts as to whether he can or should carry
the burden of divine status; essentially making

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.040
him more HUMAN.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.759
We see this push and pull as Superman decides
between being a benevolent savior or just

00:01:22.759 --> 00:01:25.909
focusing on what matters to him- namely Lois.

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:30.950
The problem is this botches a fundamental
part of having a movie about the rivalry between

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:35.580
Batman and Superman- namely, having them embody
different ideologies.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.220
This doesn’t really happen.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:43.000
Enter Batman V Superman’s biggest inspiration:
Frank Miller’s graphic novel The Dark Knight Returns.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.820
The film takes a lot of its cues from this
work: An older broken Bruce Wayne.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:47.500
Check.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.520
Superman being hit by a nuclear warhead &amp; then
being healed by the sun?

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:51.260
Oh yeah.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.460
Robin’s memorialized costume in Batman’s
lair?

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:54.140
Yep.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.220
...Even the central fight between Batman and
Superman is more-or-less taken from the graphic

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:02.880
novel -- with Superman trying to reason with
Batman, and Batman having none of it.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.240
But beneath the ‘mask’ of both The Dark
Knight Returns, and Batman v. Superman lies

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:12.980
an unlikely inspiration; one that solidifies
the all-important ideological distinction between

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.650
Batman and Superman: Ayn Rand’s The Fountainhead.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:18.090
Well, at least it does in the graphic novel.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:23.730
Rand’s novel follows Howard Roark, an ‘experimental’
architect, who faces near constant opposition

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.670
from the rigid establishment.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.519
And yeah - I know what you’re thinking:
‘What the hell do two superheroes punching

00:02:28.519 --> 00:02:30.610
each other have to do with architecture?’

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.600
Well not only has Zack Snyder been trying
to make “The Fountainhead” movie for years...

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.790
But Frank Miller cites Rand as a big influence
on his work.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:39.280
Per Miller, he was

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:53.670
In particular, both Miller and Snyder seem
drawn to Rand’s depiction of male heroes.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:57.380
In The Fountainhead, protagonist Howard Roark
is the IDEAL man.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:02.840
A man, who no matter the obstacles, achieves
his goals and never gives up an inch on his

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.959
integrity - even if that leads to conflict
with others.

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:10.590
This Randian hero is defined by “ethical
egoism” – the belief that the best course

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.159
of action is always what’s in your best
interest.

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:18.360
Only through pursuing your own self-interest,
can you ever hope to achieve happiness - a

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.230
central tenet of Ayn Rand’s philosophy:
Objectivism.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:28.420
To Rand, a male hero must be a radical individualist,
unwilling to bend, smarter than everyone else

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.970
and well, not too shabby on the eyes.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.370
Sound familiar to a certain ‘Dark Knight’?

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:37.159
Rand’s ‘Ideal Man’ fits Miller’s Batman
to a tee.

00:03:37.159 --> 00:03:42.170
He’s a super rich, handsome playboy with
a genius intellect who refuses to bend to

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.019
anyone’s will but his own.

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:49.090
It’s Batman’s adherence to Randian egoism
that enables an ideological conflict between

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:52.740
Batman and Superman- individualism over collectivism.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:10.780
Batman faces constant obstruction from the
US Government who task the ultimate ‘conformist’

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.000
to stop him: Superman.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.970
In Miller's work, Superman is the anti-Rand
figure – a character whose self-sacrificing

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:22.760
moral righteousness &amp; commitment to ‘good’
marks him a ‘Government Stooge’.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.200
Much of the drama in Miller’s graphic novel
stems from these differing ideologies: the

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.530
individualism of Batman vs. the conformism
of Superman.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:39.390
Now – whether you agree or not with Rand’s
philosophy, there’s no denying that Miller

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:44.750
successfully instilled the spirit of her theories
to create a compelling interior conflict between

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:45.750
our two titans.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:50.410
So, while Frank Miller pulled this off with
flying colors, Batman V Superman’s integration

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:53.480
of this philosophy is where it all goes wrong.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:56.260
First let’s give props to what they got
right – Batman.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:00.770
Affleck’s version is pretty much in line
with Miller’s Randian take on the character.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:07.120
He’s handsome, almost supernaturally athletic...Smarter
than everyone else...and catnip for the ladies.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.400
Batman’s also a complete individualist,
refusing to bend his will to anyone - even

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.720
the judicial process.

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:17.640
"We're criminals, Alfred. We've always been criminals. Nothing's changed.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:20.640
He even brands criminals so they'll be shanked
in prison.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.130
Now, I watched the whole movie being pretty
confused as to why Batman wanted to fight

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:29.370
Superman in the first place - but here too
I think there’s a nod to Rand.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:34.650
If Batman is so concerned about this God-like
Superman, it’s because any kind of God ruins

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:39.090
the whole ethical egoism thing: how are you
supposed to make your own choices, based on

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:43.400
your own rationality, when there’s something
objectively greater than you?

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.340
Rand was famously an atheist for this reason.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:50.070
So they get this Randian version of Batman
right – but it’s with the depiction of

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.890
Superman that things begin to get muddled.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:57.050
They couldn’t create a purely benevolent,
do-gooder Superman cuz that would be boring

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:58.940
and impossible to relate to.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:03.100
Conversely, they decide not to make him the
collectivist stooge that Miller made him,

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:07.470
because, well...the audience is supposed to
like him just as much as Batman and we’ve

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.570
got Justice League films to make people!

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:10.690
So what do they do?

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:14.730
Previous depictions of Superman have focused
on his self-sacrificing goodness.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:19.090
In Christopher Reeve’s iconic portrayal,
Superman doesn’t save people for his own

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.880
betterment, but simply because it’s the
right thing to do --

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:24.300
"I'm here to fight for truth and justice and the American way."

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.460
be it rescuing a cat from a tree, or catching Lois from plummeting to her death.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:34.560
The Superman of BvS, however, looks like he’s
stuck performing the most tiresome 9-5 job imaginable.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:39.280
Since Gods shouldn’t whine about saving
humanity, the film constantly undercuts the

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.000
perception of Superman as a purely benevolent
Christ-like hero.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.460
Which, later, Batman just straight up tells
Superman face-to-face.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:51.240
"You were never a god, you were never even a man."

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:57.240
Superman’s crisis manifests into two warring
identities - the classic Superman ‘The God’

00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:01.610
benevolent &amp; Christopher Reeves-like and
Superman ‘The Man’ -- which for Snyder

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:03.530
means a Randian Objectivist.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:08.540
IE: a guy who flies to the middle east to
save Lois but leaves all the other innocents

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:09.540
to die.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:13.040
This internal conflict is pretty bluntly stated
by Martha Kent...

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:31.020
For Martha - there’s no difference between
choosing to be a Benevolent Messiah or a Self

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:32.440
Involved Objectivist.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.240
Both are equally valid.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:38.520
The important thing is that it’s Clark’s
choice to make - and this rational choice

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:40.770
is what marks a Randian hero.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:46.000
Later the ghost of Jonathan Kent gives Clark
his own advice, revealing the time he selflessly

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.960
‘saved’ his farm from a flood; yet unbeknownst
to him – the water went upstream, flooding

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:52.700
another nearby farm.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:00.900
Jonathan Kent here dismantles the notion of
self-less heroism.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:05.690
There’s simply no point to being the ‘classic
selfless do-gooder Superman’ because there

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:08.830
will always be another flooded farm you couldn’t
stop.

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:13.310
When Clark asks Ghost dad how he could live
with himself after dooming the nearby farm,

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:14.500
he responds -

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:27.190
Dream Jonathan is kind of like a ghost of
Objectivism past: suggesting that true moral

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:31.910
righteousness comes from bettering yourself,
pursuing your own self-interest, and protecting

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:35.660
the ones you love, because they in turn bring
you happiness.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.490
Ethical egoism in a nutshell.

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:41.770
So what choice does Superman ultimately make
-- Messiah or Randian Hero?

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:44.760
Actually... he doesn’t really make a choice
at all.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:49.590
At first, Superman seems to embrace Jonathan’s
objectivism, returning to Metropolis only

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:53.260
to save the people who really matter to him
- aka Lois &amp; Martha.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:57.020
He even echoes Jonathan’s parting words,
telling Lois -

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:07.400
So what is his decision?

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:07.920
Both.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.860
As a benevolent savior, he takes responsibility
for the well being of the whole planet, but

00:09:12.861 --> 00:09:16.730
as an objectivist he also mentions he’s
doing it all for Lois.

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:22.760
Five seconds later, Superman sacrifices himself
in the most Messianic fashion possible - Christ-like

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:24.230
with his arms spread out.

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:27.900
Heck - there’s even a makeshift cross behind
Superman’s corpse.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:33.690
Superman’s ambiguous philosophy becomes
problematic since BvS is framed as an ideological

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:38.530
conflict between Batman &amp; Superman; and yet
none actually exists.

00:09:38.530 --> 00:09:43.380
The first time Bruce and Clark meet, the film
pays lip service to what SHOULD be their differing

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:44.380
beliefs...

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:48.710
Bruce doesn’t like how Metropolis and the
world blindly trusts Superman by labeling

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:49.790
him as a savior.

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:54.180
Bruce questions the very possibility of a
“superhuman benevolent savior,” because

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.920
as he’s seen- even the best can turn bad.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:00.780
"I Just have a bad history with freaks dressed like clowns."

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:04.640
And Clark thinks Batman is a vigilante, who
needs to be put in check.

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:08.780
"Civil liberties are being trampled on in your city. Good people living in fear."

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:14.140
This is a similar ideological conflict as
Miller’s The Dark Knight Returns; but BvS,

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:18.300
instead of exploring the dynamic further,
immediately upends it.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:19.960
The world turns against Superman...

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:24.460
"Headline: end of love affair with man in the sky?"

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:27.720
Because, like Batman, he acts on his own accord.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:35.860
And by using Objectivism to make Superman
more relatable, the film ends up shooting

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:40.570
itself in the foot, because now there’s
almost no ideological difference between Batman

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:41.570
and Superman.

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:46.320
So in a film that requires a conflict based
on differing ideals, that’s based on a source

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:53.180
material showcasing this ideological divide,
BvS instead becomes a ‘Randian Hero’ vs.

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:57.560
‘A Maybe Sort-of Randian Hero who can’t
decide if he wants to be the divine savior

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:59.100
the world wants him to be..’

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:02.620
So in the end, the film presents no preference
of heroics.

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:06.720
Whether one should sacrifice oneself for the
greater good, or only keep their own interests

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:11.360
in mind is immaterial, because the solution
presented isn’t that one kind of heroics

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:13.410
needs to be put in check while another promoted.

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:16.300
Rather, the message is: MORE
HEROS.

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:21.340
- Aquaman, The Flash, Cyborg - and that they
all should form their own team --

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:22.680
"Help me find the others like you."

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:24.800
aka The Justice League.

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:27.960
So instead of the film espousing a clear philosophy,
supporting Batman or Superman, everything

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:29.480
just becomes muddled.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:31.280
Quite simply -- it’s a mess.

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:35.120
But it's an amitious mess, and for that, it
deserves credit.

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:37.640
And hey -- maybe Justice League will get it
right.

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:40.680
Thanks for Wisecrack, Peace.

